iality  of the colleagues and similarly if  you're a litigant you know what should  you be asking for are you going to get  it when you're going to get it and how  does that affect how you bring your  claim I think there's going to be a huge  shift now because the principle will be  that bigger businesses should be  transparent about pay  and give us an idea of just how wide the  pay gap is at the moment how big a  problem do we have because the  government says it's at its lowest level  ever they maintain it's been virtually  eliminated for those people working  full-time under the age of 40 s what's  your take well that there are statistics  and statistics here if you look at just  full-time pay without including overtime  it's now at the lowest level it's true  it's about nine point four percent but  in this country we have a lot of  segregation between men and women in the  labor market and we have horizontal  segregation which is certain occupations  like child care say or teaching very  female dominated occupations like heavy  industry or banking very male-dominated  and then we also have vertical  stratification which means you look at a  profession like teaching a lot of women  in the junior positions very few women  at the top and that same vertical  stratification you see in professions  like the law and if you look at what the  ONS office for national statistics calls  the higher skills and trades more  skilled profession the pay gap is now 25  percent and when you look at the  financial services sector it's as high  as 55 percent well there's also a  difference between the public sector and  the private sector the public sector pay  gap now is I believe around 11% and the  average private sector pay gap is around  17% okay well hurry knee younger really  interesting to talk to you thanks very  much indeed thank you
 

